    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the weight of one bowling ball, we start by defining the variables for the weights of the bowling balls and the canoes. Let \\( B \\) represent the weight of one bowling ball and \\( C \\) represent the weight of one canoe.\n\nAccording to the problem, seven identical bowling balls weigh the same as three identical canoes. This relationship can be expressed with the equation:\n\\[ 7B = 3C \\]\n\nWe are also given that two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds. This can be written as:\n\\[ 2C = 56 \\]\n\nFirst, we solve for the weight of one canoe (\\( C \\)) by isolating \\( C \\) in the second equation:\n\\[ C = \\frac{56}{2} \\]\n\\[ C = 28 \\]\n\nNow that we know the weight of one canoe is 28 pounds, we substitute \\( C = 28 \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 7B = 3 \\times 28 \\]\n\\[ 7B = 84 \\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( B \\) by isolating \\( B \\) in the equation:\n\\[ B = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ B = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
